.TH EUCLID-CLI-EQS-EXISTS-QUEUE 1 "September 2026" "euclid-cli 1.0.53" "Euclid CLI Manual"
.SH NAME
euclid-cli-eqs-exists-queue \- whether a queue exists, as an exit code for a script
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B euclid-cli eqs exists-queue
.BR \-\-queue | \-q
.I NAME
.SH DESCRIPTION
Answers whether a queue exists. Written to be branched on rather than
read: a shell treats a successful exit as true, so the answer is the
exit status.
.PP
.B "true"
or
.B "false"
also goes to standard output, and nothing else does \(em no JSON, no
metadata, nothing to cut out of the way with
.BR sed (1).
So the same command serves
.B if
and
.BR "$(\|.\|.\|.\|)" .
.PP
The question has three answers, not two. Alongside "yes" and "no" there
is "could not tell": an expired session, an unreachable gateway, a
refused permission. Those exit
.BR 2 ,
so a script never reads an outage as an absent queue and goes on to
create one that was already there, or skip a step that mattered. A
script that only branches on
.B if
still gets the safe outcome, because
.B 2
is not success either and the true branch is not taken on an answer
nobody actually got.
.PP
Distinguishing them is the reason this exists rather than
.BR euclid-cli-eqs-get-queue-ern (1),
which exits
.B 1
both for "no such queue" and for "your session expired" and gives a
script no way to tell the two apart.
.PP
Needs the same permission as
.BR euclid-cli-eqs-get-queue-ern (1),
which is
.IR eqs:get\-queue\-ern ,
because that is the call it makes. A caller without it gets
.B 2
rather than a wrong answer.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-q ", " \-\-queue " " \fINAME\fR
Name of the queue to look for, or its ERN. Required. A missing
.B \-\-queue
is a broken command line rather than an absent queue, and exits
.BR 2 .
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The queue exists.
.B "true"
is written to standard output.
.TP
.B 1
The queue does not exist.
.B "false"
is written to standard output.
.TP
.B 2
The question could not be answered: no
.BR \-\-queue ,
an unreachable or unauthenticated gateway, a refused permission, or any
other error. The reason is written to standard error and standard output
is left empty.
.SH EXAMPLES
Create a queue only if it is not already there:
.RS
.nf
if ! euclid\-cli eqs exists\-queue \-q orders > /dev/null; then
    euclid\-cli eqs create\-queue \-\-name orders
fi
.fi
.RE
.PP
Tell the three answers apart, which a script that can act on a queue's
absence should do:
.RS
.nf
euclid\-cli eqs exists\-queue \-q orders > /dev/null
case $? in
    0) echo "already there" ;;
    1) euclid\-cli eqs create\-queue \-\-name orders ;;
    2) echo "could not tell \(em leaving it alone" >&2; exit 1 ;;
esac
.fi
.RE
.PP
Read the word instead:
.RS
.nf
if [ "$(euclid\-cli eqs exists\-queue \-q orders)" = true ]; then
    echo yes
fi
.fi
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR euclid-cli-eqs (1),
.BR euclid-cli-eqs-get-queue-ern (1),
.BR euclid-cli-eqs-create-queue (1),
.BR euclid-cli-eqs-get-queue (1)
